Text: "Complex analysis begins with the field $\mathbb{C}$ viewed
       simultaneously as an algebraic extension of $\mathbb{R}$ and as the
       Euclidean plane. A complex derivative must be independent of the
       direction of approach. That apparently small strengthening of real
       differentiability has far-reaching consequences: holomorphic functions
       are analytic, their values obey integral formulas, and their isolated
       singularities admit a complete local classification.

       The chapter develops this rigidity in stages: complex arithmetic and
       geometry, differentiability and the Cauchy--Riemann equations, contour
       integration, and finally power series, Laurent series, singularities,
       and residues. Domain hypotheses such as openness, connectedness, and
       simple connectedness are stated explicitly because the principal
       theorems are false without them."
Id: "068103dc-1280-4311-815d-fac876f55582"
